   <property name="text">
    <string>&lt;html&gt;&lt;head/&gt;&lt;body&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot; font-size:11pt; font-weight:700;&quot;&gt;Instructions:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot; font-size:11pt;&quot;&gt;&lt;br/&gt;Click the &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot; font-size:11pt; font-weight:700;&quot;&gt;Distill&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot; font-size:11pt;&quot;&gt; button to heat the fermented liquid and collect the alcohol.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot; font-size:11pt; font-weight:700;&quot;&gt;Did you know?&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot; font-size:11pt;&quot;&gt;&lt;br/&gt;Distillation separates alcohol from the fermented wash by heating it. Alcohol boils at around 78.4°C (173°F), allowing it to evaporate before water and other compounds. The vapor is then condensed back into liquid form, resulting in a stronger and purer spirit. This process increases the alcohol content from approximately 7–10% after fermentation to about 65–75% after distillation. Different distillation methods, such as pot stills and column stills, influence the rum's final flavor and strength. ​ &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/body&gt;&lt;/html&gt;</string>
